   "text": "This is phylloquinone (vitamin K1): a 2-methyl-1,4-naphthoquinone carrying a phytyl side chain at C3.\n\n**Ring.** The quinone ring is fused to a benzene ring. Its two C=O carbons sit at the positions drawn with the O labels, and one ring carbon beside them carries a methyl group. The adjacent ring carbon carries the side chain.\n\n**Side chain.** Reading from the ring outward, I counted:\n- a CH2, then a CH= double-bonded to a carbon bearing a methyl;\n- three CH2 groups, then a stereocentre with a hashed methyl;\n- three CH2 groups, then a stereocentre with a wedged methyl;\n- three CH2 groups, then a terminal isopropyl group.\n\n**Double bond.** The methyl on the quaternary alkene carbon and the ring-side CH2 on the other alkene carbon both point up. So the two main-chain CH2 groups are on opposite sides, which makes the double bond E.\n\n**Stereocentres.** I assigned each centre from the wedge or hash and the implicit H, writing the SMILES from the isopropyl end:\n- The wedged-methyl carbon comes out as `[C@@H]`.\n- The hashed-methyl carbon also comes out as `[C@@H]`.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)CCC[C@@H](C)CCC[C@@H](C)CCC/C(C)=C/CC1=C(C)C(=O)c2ccccc2C1=O"
